PART 1 — LOGIN TO AWS
Step 1: Open AWS Console
Open Chrome
Search:
AWS Console
Login with your AWS account

After login, you will see:

Search bar at top
AWS services
PART 2 — CREATE UBUNTU LINUX EC2 INSTANCE
Step 2: Open EC2 Service
Where is EC2 button?

At top search bar type:

EC2

Click:

🟧 EC2
Step 3: Launch Instance

After opening EC2:

Left side menu:

Click:

Instances

Now click orange button:

Launch Instances

(Top right side)

PART 3 — CONFIGURE UBUNTU INSTANCE
Step 4: Give Instance Name

You will see:

Name and tags

Inside Name box type:

UbuntuServer
Step 5: Choose Operating System

Scroll little down.

You will see:

Application and OS Images (AMI)

Select:

✅ Ubuntu

Step 6: Choose Instance Type

Scroll little down.

You will see:

Instance Type

Choose:

t2.micro

(Free tier)

PART 4 — CREATE KEY PAIR
Step 7: Create Key Pair

Scroll down.

You will see:

Key pair (login)

Click:

Create new key pair

A popup opens.

Fill details:
Key pair name:
mykey
Key pair type:

Keep default

Private key file format:

Choose:

.pem

Click:

Create key pair

IMPORTANT:
File downloads automatically.

Example:

mykey.pem

Keep this safely.

PART 5 — NETWORK SETTINGS
Step 8: Allow SSH

Scroll down.

You will see:

Network settings

Click:

Edit

Ensure this checkbox is selected:

✅ Allow SSH traffic from

Choose:

My IP

SSH means Linux connection.

Port used:

22
PART 6 — LAUNCH INSTANCE
Step 9: Launch

Now click orange button:

Launch Instance

(Right side)

Wait 1 minute.

You will see:

✅ Successfully launched

PART 7 — CONNECT USING PuTTY

(Windows users)

STEP A — INSTALL PuTTY

Open browser.

Search:

PuTTY download

Open:

PuTTY Official Website

Download:

PuTTY
PuTTYgen

Install both.

STEP B — CONVERT .pem TO .ppk

PuTTY does NOT use .pem directly.

We convert:

.pem → .ppk
Step 1: Open PuTTYgen

Press Windows key.

Search:

PuTTYgen

Open it.

Step 2: Load PEM File

Inside PuTTYgen:

Click:

Load

Bottom right change:

All Files (*.*)

Select:

mykey.pem

Click:

Open
Step 3: Save PPK File

Click:

Save private key

Save as:

mykey.ppk

Done.

PART 8 — GET PUBLIC IP
Step 1

Go back to AWS EC2.

Click:

Instances

Select your Ubuntu instance.

Step 2

Below, you will see:

Public IPv4 address

OR

Public DNS

Copy it.

Example:

3.110.xxx.xxx
PART 9 — OPEN PuTTY
Step 1: Open PuTTY

Search:

PuTTY

Open it.

You will see:

PuTTY Configuration
Step 2: Enter Host Name

Inside:

Host Name (or IP address)

Type:

ubuntu@YOUR_PUBLIC_IP

Example:

ubuntu@3.110.xxx.xxx
PART 10 — ATTACH PPK FILE
Step 1

Left side menu:

Click:

Connection

Then:

SSH

Then:

Auth

Then:

Credentials
Step 2

You will see:

Private key file for authentication

Click:

Browse

Select:

mykey.ppk

Click:

Open
PART 11 — CONNECT TO UBUNTU

Now click:

Open

(At bottom)

First Time Security Popup

A warning appears.

Click:

Yes
SUCCESS

Black terminal opens.

You are now connected to Ubuntu Linux EC2. 🎉

VERIFY CONNECTION

Type:

whoami

Press ENTER.

Output should be:

ubuntu

PART 12 — CONNECT USING AWS CLOUDSHELL
Step 1

Top right corner in AWS Console.

Find terminal icon:

>_

This is:

CloudShell

Click it.

Step 2

Wait for terminal to open.

Step 3

Go to:

EC2 → Instances

Select Ubuntu instance.

Click:

Connect
Step 4

Open tab:

SSH Client

Copy SSH command.

Looks like:

ssh -i "mykey.pem" ubuntu@ec2-xx-xx.compute.amazonaws.com
Step 5

Paste into CloudShell.

It FAILS first time because key file not uploaded.

Step 6 — Upload PEM File

In CloudShell top right:

Click:

Actions

OR

+

Click:

Upload file

Upload:

mykey.pem
Step 7

Paste SSH command again.

Press ENTER.

Connected successfully. 🎉

PART 13 — CREATE WINDOWS EC2 INSTANCE
Step 1

Go:

EC2 → Launch Instance
Step 2 — Name

Type:

WindowsServer
Step 3 — Choose Windows

Under:

Application and OS Images

Choose:

✅ Windows

Example:

Windows Server 2022
Step 4 — Instance Type

Choose:

t2.micro
Step 5 — Key Pair

Create/select key pair.

Download .pem file.

Step 6 — Allow RDP

In:

Network settings

Ensure:

✅ Allow RDP traffic

Port:

3389

Source:

My IP
Step 7 — Launch

Click:

Launch Instance

PART 14 — GET WINDOWS PASSWORD
Step 1

Go:

EC2 → Instances

Select Windows instance.

Step 2

Click:

Connect
Step 3

Open tab:

RDP Client
Step 4

Click:

Get Password
Step 5

Click:

Upload private key file

Upload:

mykey.pem
Step 6

Click:

Decrypt Password

You will see:

Username
Password

Copy password.

PART 15 — CONNECT USING RDP
Step 1

Press:

Windows + R

Type:

mstsc

Press ENTER.

(Remote Desktop opens)

Step 2

In:

Computer

Paste:

Public IP

Click:

Connect
Step 3

Login details:

Username:
Administrator
Password:

Paste decrypted password.

Step 4

Click:

OK

Windows desktop opens. 🎉
